---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Finance"
    categorySlug="guides/finance"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">Getting Paid on Time Starts Here</h2>
  <p>
    Payment terms are one of the most important—and most overlooked—parts of freelance business.
    The terms you set determine when money arrives in your account, and the wrong terms can create
    serious cash flow problems.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Net 30 has become the industry default, but it shouldn't be.</strong> For many freelancers,
    waiting 30+ days for payment (often longer when clients pay late) creates unnecessary stress.
    Understanding your options helps you choose terms that protect your business.
  </p>

  <!-- Common Payment Terms -->
  <h2 id="payment-terms">Common Payment Terms Explained</h2>

  <h3>Due on Receipt (DOR)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Meaning:</strong> Payment expected immediately upon receiving the invoice</li>
    <li><strong>Reality:</strong> Most clients pay within 7-14 days</li>
    <li><strong>Best for:</strong> New clients, small projects, retail/consumer services</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Net 7 / Net 10</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Meaning:</strong> Payment due within 7 or 10 days</li>
    <li><strong>Best for:</strong> Ongoing relationships, smaller invoices, service businesses</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Net 15</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Meaning:</strong> Payment due within 15 days</li>
    <li><strong>Best for:</strong> Balanced approach—reasonable for clients, good for cash flow</li>
    <li><strong>Often:</strong> A good middle ground for freelancers</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Net 30</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Meaning:</strong> Payment due within 30 days</li>
    <li><strong>Best for:</strong> Established relationships, larger companies, B2B standard</li>
    <li><strong>Warning:</strong> Industry default doesn't mean it's right for you</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Net 45 / Net 60 / Net 90</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Meaning:</strong> Extended payment windows (45, 60, or 90 days)</li>
    <li><strong>Best for:</strong> Large enterprises with accounting cycles</li>
    <li><strong>Reality:</strong> Rarely appropriate for freelancers unless compensated</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Net 30 vs Net 15 -->
  <h2 id="net-30-vs-15">Net 30 vs Net 15 Comparison</h2>
  <p>
    Choosing between Net 30 and Net 15 significantly impacts your cash flow.
  </p>

  <h3>Net 15 Advantages</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Faster cash flow:</strong> Money arrives 2 weeks sooner</li>
    <li><strong>Less financing needed:</strong> Reduced need for credit or savings buffer</li>
    <li><strong>Better collection rates:</strong> Shorter terms often mean better payment behavior</li>
    <li><strong>Signals professionalism:</strong> Shows you run a serious business</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Net 30 Advantages</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Client-friendly:</strong> More time to process invoices</li>
    <li><strong>Industry expectation:</strong> Standard in many B2B relationships</li>
    <li><strong>Larger projects:</strong> Reasonable for invoices over $5,000</li>
    <li><strong>Enterprise clients:</strong> Some large companies require it</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Real Impact on Cash Flow</h3>
  <p>
    With $10,000 in monthly billings:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Net 15:</strong> You typically have ~$5,000-7,500 of receivables outstanding</li>
    <li><strong>Net 30:</strong> You typically have ~$10,000-15,000 outstanding</li>
    <li><strong>Net 60:</strong> You might have ~$20,000+ waiting to come in</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The difference compounds when you have multiple clients on different cycles.
  </p>

  <!-- Due on Receipt -->
  <h2 id="due-on-receipt">When to Use Due on Receipt</h2>
  <p>
    Due on Receipt isn't aggressive—it's appropriate for many situations:
  </p>

  <h3>Good Candidates for DOR</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>New clients:</strong> Until they prove reliable payment behavior</li>
    <li><strong>One-time projects:</strong> No ongoing relationship to maintain</li>
    <li><strong>Small invoices:</strong> Under $500-1,000</li>
    <li><strong>Consumer clients:</strong> Individuals (vs. businesses)</li>
    <li><strong>Rush work:</strong> When you prioritized their project</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How to Frame It</h3>
  <p>
    Don't apologize for DOR terms. Professional language:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>"Payment is due upon receipt of this invoice."</li>
    <li>"Please remit payment within 7 days."</li>
    <li>"We accept [payment methods]. Payment is due immediately."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Most clients won't push back—and those who do may be warning signs.
  </p>

  <!-- Early Payment Discounts -->
  <h2 id="early-payment">Early Payment Discounts (2/10 Net 30)</h2>
  <p>
    Early payment discounts incentivize faster payment with a small price reduction.
  </p>

  <h3>Common Discount Structures</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>2/10 Net 30:</strong> 2% discount if paid within 10 days, otherwise full amount due in 30</li>
    <li><strong>1/10 Net 30:</strong> 1% discount for payment within 10 days</li>
    <li><strong>3/15 Net 45:</strong> 3% discount if paid within 15 days, full amount in 45</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The Math Behind Discounts</h3>
  <p>
    A 2/10 Net 30 discount means clients save 2% by paying 20 days early.
    Annualized, that's roughly:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>2% ÷ 20 days × 365 days = ~36.5% annual return</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Clients with strong cash positions should always take this discount—it's better than
    any investment return. For you, it's paying 36% interest to get cash 20 days early.
  </p>

  <h3>When Discounts Make Sense</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>You need cash urgently (but consider if this is a recurring problem)</li>
    <li>You're working with slow-paying clients who respond to incentives</li>
    <li>Your margins are high enough to absorb the discount</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>When to Skip Discounts</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Your cash flow is healthy</li>
    <li>Clients already pay on time</li>
    <li>Margins are thin</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Late Payment Fees -->
  <h2 id="late-fees">Late Payment Fees</h2>
  <p>
    Late fees discourage delayed payment and compensate you for the inconvenience.
  </p>

  <h3>Standard Late Fee Structures</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Percentage-based:</strong> 1-1.5% per month (12-18% annually)</li>
    <li><strong>Flat fee:</strong> $25-50 per late invoice</li>
    <li><strong>Combination:</strong> Flat fee + percentage for continued lateness</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How to Implement</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>State terms clearly:</strong> On invoices, contracts, and statements of work</li>
    <li><strong>Get agreement:</strong> Client should acknowledge terms before work begins</li>
    <li><strong>Apply consistently:</strong> Don't waive for some and not others</li>
    <li><strong>Communicate first:</strong> Send reminder before applying fee</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Sample Language</h3>
  <p>
    "Payments not received within [X] days of invoice date are subject to a late fee of 1.5% per
    month (18% annually). We reserve the right to suspend work on ongoing projects until outstanding
    balances are cleared."
  </p>

  <h3>The Psychology of Late Fees</h3>
  <p>
    The goal isn't to collect late fees—it's to encourage on-time payment. Many freelancers
    include late fee language but rarely enforce it. The mere presence of the policy improves
    payment behavior.
  </p>

  <!-- Choosing Terms by Client -->
  <h2 id="client-terms">Choosing Terms by Client Type</h2>

  <h3>Small Businesses & Startups</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Recommended:</strong> Net 15 or 50% deposit + Net 15 on completion</li>
    <li><strong>Why:</strong> Cash flow is often tight; shorter terms help both parties</li>
    <li><strong>Risk:</strong> Higher risk of payment issues—use deposits</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Mid-Size Companies</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Recommended:</strong> Net 15 to Net 30</li>
    <li><strong>Why:</strong> Usually have accounts payable processes but not bureaucratic</li>
    <li><strong>Tip:</strong> Build relationship with AP contact for smoother payments</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Enterprise Clients</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Recommended:</strong> Net 30 (may require Net 45-60)</li>
    <li><strong>Why:</strong> Large companies have fixed payment cycles and processes</li>
    <li><strong>Tip:</strong> Price in the longer payment cycle; request deposit if possible</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Individual Consumers</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Recommended:</strong> Due on Receipt or prepayment</li>
    <li><strong>Why:</strong> No accounts payable department; payment is personal decision</li>
    <li><strong>Tip:</strong> Accept credit cards for immediate payment</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Communication Tips -->
  <h2 id="communication">How to Communicate Terms</h2>

  <h3>In Your Contract</h3>
  <p>
    Payment terms should appear in every contract:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Deposit requirements (amount, when due)</li>
    <li>Payment schedule for milestones</li>
    <li>Final payment terms</li>
    <li>Late fee policy</li>
    <li>Accepted payment methods</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>On Your Invoice</h3>
  <p>
    Clear invoice language:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>"Payment Terms: Net 15"</li>
    <li>"Due Date: [Specific Date]"</li>
    <li>"Please pay by: [Date]"</li>
    <li>Late fee policy reminder</li>
    <li>Payment instructions and methods</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>During Sales Conversations</h3>
  <p>
    Mention terms before they sign:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>"We require a 50% deposit to begin and the balance due upon completion."</li>
    <li>"Our standard terms are Net 15 from invoice date."</li>
    <li>"Do these payment terms work for your process?"</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Bringing it up naturally prevents surprises and shows professionalism.
  </p>

  <!-- Handling Late Payments -->
  <h2 id="late-payments">What to Do When Clients Pay Late</h2>

  <h3>Gentle Reminder (Day 1-7 Past Due)</h3>
  <p>
    "Hi [Name], I wanted to follow up on invoice #XXX dated [date]. Our records show it's past due.
    Could you let me know when to expect payment? Happy to resend the invoice if needed."
  </p>

  <h3>Firmer Follow-Up (Day 8-14)</h3>
  <p>
    "Hi [Name], Following up on invoice #XXX which is now [X] days past due. Please remit payment
    at your earliest convenience. Per our agreement, late fees of [X%] will apply to balances
    over [X] days."
  </p>

  <h3>Final Notice (Day 15-30)</h3>
  <p>
    "Dear [Name], Invoice #XXX for $[amount] is now [X] days overdue. Payment is required within
    7 days to avoid [late fees / work suspension / collection action]. Please contact me immediately
    if there's an issue."
  </p>

  <h3>When to Escalate</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>30+ days:</strong> Phone call to discuss directly</li>
    <li><strong>60+ days:</strong> Formal demand letter</li>
    <li><strong>90+ days:</strong> Consider collections or legal action</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Prevention Is Better</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Vet clients before accepting work</li>
    <li>Require deposits on new relationships</li>
    <li>Set up automatic payment reminders</li>
    <li>Invoice promptly—delays on your end delay their payment</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <h2>Conclusion: Take Control of Your Cash Flow</h2>
  <p>
    Payment terms aren't just administrative details—they're business decisions that affect
    your financial health. The right terms protect your cash flow while maintaining good
    client relationships.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Key takeaways:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Net 30 isn't mandatory—choose terms that work for your business</li>
    <li>Shorter terms (Net 15 or DOR) often make more sense for freelancers</li>
    <li>Always require deposits from new clients</li>
    <li>State terms clearly in contracts and invoices</li>
    <li>Have a system for following up on late payments</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You did the work. You deserve to get paid—on time.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
